  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (ID `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) covers `kibana_sample_data_flights`. Its time field is `timestamp`. I looked up its fields and used `Carrier` (string) and `Cancelled` (boolean) below.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-2b7146d9 Flights' (ID `2dc905a4-7dac-43f6-835d-59bc87c1012b`) has exactly two panels on that data view. One is a line chart of flight count over `timestamp`, and the other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`, showing the top 10.\n3. **Rule:** 'S4-2b7146d9 Cancelled flights' (ID `f5485a1d-f14a-4271-8a3f-d0a7cfac0221`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute, counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over the last 5 minutes, and fires when the count is above 10. It has no actions attached, so it will not notify anyone. I created it with `enabled: true`, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel was first titled 'Carrier'. I renamed it 'Flights by carrier' by updating the whole dashboard, and the response shows the two panel titles as 'Flights over time' and 'Flights by carrier'. Reading the rule back returned `enabled: true`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
